      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I entered the order as ORD-0001: ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth, four times daily.\n\nThis dose totals 3,200 mg/day, which is the usual prescription maximum for ibuprofen. It's within the limit for your patient, who has normal kidney and liver function and no allergies. Here are some things to consider:\n- **Administration:** Give it with food or milk to reduce GI upset.\n- **Duration:** Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time. Many clinicians start lower, such as 400\u2013600 mg every 6\u20138 hours, and increase only if needed.\n- **Monitoring:** Watch for GI bleeding, rising blood pressure, fluid retention, and any change in kidney function. Check for anticoagulants, antiplatelets, steroids, or ACE inhibitors/ARBs/diuretics on the med list, because these raise the risk.\n- **Gastroprotection:** Consider a PPI if the patient has GI risk factors.\n\nPlease confirm the plan with your supervising resident or attending as needed."
      }
     ],
